.Dd September 29, 2026
.Dt ZFS-EVENTS 8
.Os
.
.Sh NAME
.Nm zfs-events
.Nd display file-level event history for ZFS datasets
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm zfs
.Cm events
.Op Fl cj
.Op Fl n Ar count
.Op Fl o Ar object
.Ar filesystem
.Op Ar path
.
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm zfs Cm events
command displays the file-level event history for a ZFS dataset.
Events are logged when files and directories are created, modified, renamed,
or deleted, providing a complete audit trail of filesystem changes.
.Pp
Events are stored in an internal ring buffer within each dataset and are
keyed by ZFS object ID, ensuring that the history of a file is preserved
even after renames.
The event log is included in ZFS send streams and travels with the pool,
making it useful for replication conflict detection and forensic analysis.
.Pp
To enable event logging on a dataset, set the
.Sy events
property:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfs set events=on tank/data
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Sy events
property is inheritable, so enabling it on a parent dataset will enable
event logging for all descendant datasets.
.
.Ss Event Types
The following event types are recorded:
.Bl -tag -width "TRUNCATE" -compact -offset indent
.It Sy CREATE
File or directory created
.It Sy REMOVE
File or directory deleted
.It Sy RENAME
File or directory renamed (includes old and new paths)
.It Sy LINK
Hard link created
.It Sy SYMLINK
Symbolic link created
.It Sy TRUNCATE
File truncated
.It Sy SETATTR
File attributes changed (permissions, ownership, times)
.It Sy WRITE
File content written (requires
.Sy events_io Ns = Ns Sy on )
.It Sy READ
File content read (requires
.Sy events_io Ns = Ns Sy on )
.El
.Pp
.Sy WRITE
and
.Sy READ
events are recorded only when the
.Sy events_io
property is set to
.Sy on ;
see
.Sx IO AUDITING
below.
.
.Sh IO AUDITING
In addition to the metadata operations above,
.Nm zfs Cm events
can record file content access as
.Sy WRITE
and
.Sy READ
events.
IO auditing is disabled by default and is enabled per dataset:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfs set events_io=on tank/data
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Sy events
property must be set to
.Sy on
for IO auditing to have an effect.
Each
.Sy WRITE
or
.Sy READ
record carries
.Sy io_offset
and
.Sy io_bytes
in addition to the common fields; in JSON output they appear as
.Sy io_offset
and
.Sy io_bytes
members.
.Pp
IO events are coalesced by a per-file, per-direction time fence
controlled by the
.Sy events_io_window
property (milliseconds, 0 to 3600000, default 1000).
While the window is open, I/O to the same file in the same direction
is absorbed into a single pending record: byte counts are summed into
.Sy io_bytes ,
and
.Sy io_offset
is the window's first offset.
Bytes are coalesced, never dropped: every byte written or read while
the window is open appears in exactly one record's
.Sy io_bytes .
The window closes on the next I/O past the window, on any non-IO
event for the same file (records keep cause order, so a
.Sy WRITE
followed by a
.Sy RENAME
is emitted in that order), and on
.Xr close 2 .
.Xr close 2
flushes a pending window even when the window is still young, so a
record does not wait on inode eviction.
A window of
.Sy 0
has no pending record: each
.Xr read 2
or
.Xr write 2
is queued on its own and drained asynchronously.
.Pp
The
.Sy uid
and
.Sy gid
of a coalesced record are those of the caller that opened the window,
not the caller that later flushed it.
Setting
.Sy events_io_window=0
disables the fence and records every
.Xr write 2
and
.Xr read 2
call individually.
.Pp
A coalesced
.Sy WRITE
carries the pool's open transaction group at flush time, not the
transaction group of the write that filled the window.
A
.Sy READ
record, and a
.Sy events_io_window=0
record, carries the open transaction group sampled when the record
was queued.
Reads create no transaction of their own, so this value is a
commit-timeline anchor, not a claim that the read was part of that
transaction group.
.Pp
Limitations:
.Bl -bullet -compact -offset indent
.It
Writes performed through
.Xr mmap 2
bypass the VFS read/write paths and generate no IO events.
.It
At
.Sy events_io_window=0 ,
sustained bulk I/O emits a record per syscall (roughly 1024 records
per GiB with 1M block size) and can wrap a small event ring
continuously; size
.Sy events_size
accordingly or use a nonzero window, which emits at most one record
per window period.
.El
.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "-o object"
.It Fl c
Clear the dataset's event log.
Requires a writable pool and the
.Sy destroy
delegation permission.
The log's ring identity is preserved; the logical end of the log
resets to 0.
.It Fl j
Output one JSON array.
Field names are the wire names
.Pq Sy txg , object , op , name ,
and the operation-specific fields.
The kernel
.Sy time
field is not printed.
.It Fl n Ar count
Stop after
.Ar count
records, counting from the oldest surviving record.
This is not a "most recent" selector.
By default, all events in the ring buffer are displayed.
.It Fl o Ar object
Display only events for the specified ZFS object ID.
Object IDs can be obtained from
.Xr zdb 8
or from previous
.Nm zfs Cm events
output.
.It Ar filesystem
The dataset to query.
Volumes and snapshots are not accepted.
.It Ar path
Optional path of a live file.
The command stats the path and filters by that inode's object ID.
An absolute path is used as given; a relative path is joined to the
dataset mountpoint.
A path that no longer exists cannot be filtered, including a rename's
old name.
.El
.
.Sh OUTPUT
Text output columns are
.Sy TXG ,
.Sy OBJECT ,
.Sy OPERATION ,
and
.Sy NAME .
A rename appends
.Dq (from Ar oldname )
with the bare previous name, not a full path.
JSON output is a single array of objects using the wire field names.
.Sy WRITE
and
.Sy READ
objects additionally carry
.Sy io_offset
and
.Sy io_bytes .
.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Ss Example 1: Viewing events on a dataset
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfs events tank/data
TXG        OBJECT   OPERATION  NAME
---------- -------- ---------- --------------------
1847       128      CREATE     newfile.txt
1850       128      RENAME     renamed.txt (from newfile.txt)
.Ed
.Ss Example 2: JSON output
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfs events -j -n 1 tank/data
[{"txg":1847,"object":128,"op":"CREATE","name":"newfile.txt"}]
.Ed
.Pp
.Fl n
counts from the oldest surviving record, so this is the oldest record,
not the newest.
.Ss Example 3: IO auditing
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfs set events_io=on tank/data
# zfs set events_io_window=5000 tank/data
# dd if=/dev/zero of=/tank/data/blob bs=1M count=64
# zfs events -j -o 130 tank/data
[{"txg":1861,"object":130,"op":"WRITE","name":"blob",
 "io_offset":0,"io_bytes":67108864}]
.Ed
.
.Sh INTERFACE STABILITY
.Sy Evolving .
.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr zfs 8 ,
.Xr zfs-diff 8 ,
.Xr zfs-set 8 ,
.Xr zfsprops 7 ,
.Xr zmetad 8 ,
.Xr zpool-features 7
.
.Sh NOTES
The event log is stored as a ring buffer within the dataset.
When the buffer is full, the oldest events are overwritten.
The size of the buffer is fixed when the log object is created, from
the
.Sy events_size
property at that moment (default 1 MiB).
Changing
.Sy events_size
later does not resize an existing log.
.Pp
Metadata operations are recorded in the same transaction group as the
operation.
Coalesced IO records are not: their transaction group is the pool's
open group at flush or enqueue time.
.Pp
The
.Sy org.openzfs:events
pool feature must be enabled before
.Sy events=on
is accepted.
The feature becomes
.Sy active
when the first log object is created, which is the first event, not
the property set itself.
.Sy events=off
does not destroy the log and does not return the feature to
.Sy enabled .
It is refused while
.Sy events_io
is still
.Sy on .
The feature returns to
.Sy enabled
when every dataset that created a log object is destroyed.
.Pp
For long-term event retention beyond the ring buffer capacity, use
.Xr zmetad 8
to export events to an external SQLite database.
